  Spec written to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-30-login-tracking-design.md[39m (not committed). Please review it before I
  write the implementation plan.

  [1mThe headline, in case it's buried:[0m I'm not adding a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter. Your answer that the server produces the ID
  means [38;5;153muserId[39m belongs in [38;5;153mlogin[39m's [1mreturn value[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m keeps its signature. If you expected a
  parameter to appear in the diff, that's the disagreement to resolve now, not after the plan.

  Three things in the spec worth your attention specifically:

  1. [1mThe stub returns a deliberately fake ID[0m ([38;5;153m"stub-user-id"[39m), not a plausible one derived from the username. A
     realistic-looking fake ID is the kind of thing that reaches a real analytics backend and quietly pollutes it.
  2. [1mFailed logins are not tracked.[0m You picked [38;5;153muserId[39m/[38;5;153musername[39m/[38;5;153mtimestamp[39m without success-or-failure, so the seam only
     fires on success. That's a coherent choice, but it means failed attempts leave no trace and credential-stuffing
     won't be visible. Easy to add later; flagging it because it's a silent gap rather than a visible one.
  3. [1m[38;5;153m.mjs[39m files instead of [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m.[0m The conventional move would break [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m — the
     unrelated [38;5;153mgreet[39m demo — so I avoided touching them. The cost is that a static server has to serve [38;5;153m.mjs[39m with a JS
     MIME type ([38;5;153mnpx serve[39m does; some don't).

  One scope note: switching [38;5;153mindex.html[39m to [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m means the page no longer opens by double-clicking the file. You
  approved that tradeoff, but it's the change most likely to surprise you in practice.

  Nothing has been implemented — no source files touched. Once you approve the spec, the next step is the writing-plans
  skill to turn it into an implementation plan.

